    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery right now and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Every time I try to cut the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering at the tips of the pins, even though I'm being careful with the layout. It's compromising the fit and making the joints look messy. Can you give me some advice on how to manage the grain direction better or what I should check with my chisel sharpness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splintering at the tips is often caused by your grain direction running too closely to the cut line rather than squarely through the end. You should try ripping your stock so the grain stands perpendicular to the tangs of the pins, ensuring a clean break when you cut. Additionally, verify that your chisels are razor-sharp; dull tools struggle to slice wood cleanly and will definitely crush the fibers. Finally, reduce your starting depth slightly on the second cut to let the tangent knife clear the previous \"V\" deeply before applying more blade.",
      "source": "target",
