    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i've been honing the chisels on a strop regularly so i'm pretty sure the edge is fine, but i'll double check. could you go into more detail about the grain management? i'm wondering if the moisture content in the walnut might be playing a role in the splintering, or if there's a specific way i should be orienting my cuts to avoid the fibers tearing away at the very end of the pin.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While moisture content can cause walnut to warp or shrink unexpectedly, it rarely causes clean fiber tearing at the cut unless the wood is environmentally stressed. The primary issue is almost certainly the angle of the grain relative to the cut, where a 45-degree angle at the tail creates a weak path for the tool to sever fibers. You likely need to adjust your saw cuts to square the entry point of your chisel with the end grain, allowing a full, clean sine-cut before pulling the tail through. Once the entry is perfect, you can apply a slightly steeper final push, and chipping away minimal ragged wood will prevent the messy splinters.",
